    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "The 1936 Literary Digest Election Disaster",
     "content": "In 1936, *Literary Digest* magazine mailed **10 million** ballots predicting the U.S. presidential election. Its sample was the largest poll in history. It confidently called the race for **Alf Landon**.\n\nFranklin D. Roosevelt won 46 of 48 states.\n\nThe problem wasn't sample size — it was sample selection:\n\n• Ballots were mailed to people in **phone books, magazine subscriber lists, and auto registries**\n• In the depths of the Great Depression, those groups skewed **wealthy and Republican-leaning**\n• Of those who received ballots, only **22% responded** — and angry, motivated Landon supporters were more likely to send theirs back\n\nMeanwhile, an unknown pollster named **George Gallup** asked just 50,000 *demographically representative* voters and called the election correctly.\n\nThe lesson: a giant self-selected sample is worse than a small, properly drawn one. Quantity does not fix selection bias.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">1936 ELECTION POLLING</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Size doesn't fix sampling</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"440\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">LITERARY DIGEST</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">10,000,000 ballots</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">22% response rate</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Wealthy mailing lists</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">PREDICTED: Landon</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Wrong by 38 percentage pts</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"180\" width=\"440\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">GALLUP POLL</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">50,000 respondents</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Quota-sampled</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Representative groups</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">PREDICTED: Roosevelt</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Within 1 point of result</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"560\" width=\"740\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"615\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">THE LESSON</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"665\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">A 200x smaller, properly drawn sample</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">beat a self-selected mega-sample</text></svg>",
      "caption": "How sampling works decides whether sampling works"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Outcome Bias: Judging Decisions by What Happened",
     "content": "**Outcome bias** is the tendency to evaluate the *quality of a decision* based on how the decision *turned out*, instead of how reasonable it was at the time given the information available.\n\nDobelli's example: a surgeon performs a routine operation with a 2% mortality rate. The patient dies. People conclude the surgeon was negligent. If the patient had lived, no one would have asked. **Same decision. Same skill. Same probabilities. Different verdict.**\n\nWhy it matters in practice:\n\n• **Investors** who got rich during a bubble are crowned 'visionaries'\n• **Gamblers** who win at roulette feel like they had a 'system'\n• **Executives** who launched a risky product that succeeded are called 'bold'; if it failed, 'reckless'\n• **Pilots** who land in a storm safely are praised; in fact, the right call may have been to divert\n\nThe defense is **process auditing**: judge a decision by the information, options, and expected values *at the moment of choice*, not by the lucky or unlucky result that followed. Good decisions can have bad outcomes. Bad decisions can have good outcomes. Pretending otherwise warps every lesson you draw.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">OUTCOME BIAS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Decision quality ≠ outcome quality</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"180\" width=\"900\" height=\"100\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">SAME DECISION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Surgery with 98% survival rate</text><path d=\"M400 290 L320 350\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"312,348 328,342 324,358\" fill=\"#10b981\"/><path d=\"M700 290 L780 350\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"788,348 772,342 776,358\" fill=\"#10b981\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"370\" width=\"440\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">PATIENT SURVIVES</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"470\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Brilliant surgeon\"</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Great decision\"</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"555\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Reputation rises</text><rect x=\"600\" y=\"370\" width=\"440\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"820\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">PATIENT DIES</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"470\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Should have known\"</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Negligent decision\"</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"555\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Lawsuits, blame</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"630\" width=\"740\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"685\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">AUDIT THE PROCESS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"730\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Not the outcome. The choice was identical</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"765\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">at the moment it was made.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Outcomes are noisy — decisions can be evaluated cleanly"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "The 2008 Mortgage Heroes and Villains",
     "content": "Before September 2008, two CEOs ran investment banks with **nearly identical** strategies — same leverage ratios, same mortgage-backed exposure, same risk models.\n\nOne firm's exposure was hedged just before Lehman fell. The CEO was called **'the smartest man on Wall Street'** and wrote a memoir.\n\nThe other firm's identical positions blew up by Friday. The CEO was hauled before Congress, sued personally, and labeled **'reckless and incompetent.'**\n\nAfterward, financial historians like Andrew Lo and Nassim Taleb pointed out: the **decision processes** had been nearly indistinguishable, separated by **luck of timing** in the unwind. The hero and villain stories were almost entirely outcome bias — applied to people who had taken the *same* gambles.\n\nThis matters in your life too. If you praise your investment decisions when stocks go up and curse them when stocks go down, you are training yourself to learn the **wrong** lessons. The market is noisy. Your decision process is the only thing you control.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">2008: HERO OR VILLAIN?</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Identical decisions, opposite legacies</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"180\" width=\"900\" height=\"100\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">JUNE 2008</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Two CEOs, nearly identical leverage</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"320\" width=\"440\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"370\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">CEO A: GOT OUT IN TIME</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Memoir on the bestseller list</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"460\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Speaking fee: $200k/talk</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Joins board of every fund</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">\"He saw it coming\"</text><rect x=\"600\" y=\"320\" width=\"440\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"820\" y=\"370\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">CEO B: HIT BY LEHMAN</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Congressional testimony</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"460\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Personal lawsuits</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Industry exile</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">\"He should have known\"</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"640\" width=\"740\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"695\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">THE CRUEL TRUTH</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"740\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Same playbook. Different week.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"775\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Outcome wrote the morality tale.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Hero vs. villain is often just timing"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "Sara picks a stock based on careful analysis with an 80% expected return over 3 years. It loses 15%. Which evaluation reflects clear thinking *about her decision*?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "She made a bad decision because she lost money",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "She made a great decision because losing money is unusual",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The outcome doesn't decide the quality of her process — we have to audit her analysis at the time of choice",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "She should have known the market would fall",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "**Outcome bias** judges the choice by the result. Clean evaluation separates the two: was the analysis sound, were probabilities estimated reasonably, were risks sized correctly? A well-reasoned bet can still lose; a sloppy bet can still win. To **learn** from decisions, audit the process — the inputs, options, and reasoning at the moment of choice.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Process vs. Outcome</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"200\" width=\"440\" height=\"240\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.1)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">DECISION QUALITY</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Information at the time</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Process used</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Risks considered</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"200\" width=\"440\" height=\"240\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.1)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">OUTCOME QUALITY</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Includes luck</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"360\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Includes noise</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Sometimes unrelated</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"490\" width=\"740\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">EVALUATE WHAT YOU CONTROL</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">You control your process</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"630\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">You do not control the result</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Audit the process, not the dice"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "Three De-biasing Habits You Can Start Today",
     "content": "Each of these targets one of today's biases:\n\n1. **Hunt the cemetery (survivorship)** — Before adopting any 'success habit,' search for sources on people who failed despite using it. If the failures used it too, the habit may be irrelevant.\n\n2. **Ask 'who isn't here?' (self-selection)** — On every survey, review, or testimonial, ask which people are systematically *missing* from the dataset and whether their absence skews the picture.\n\n3. **Keep a decision journal (outcome bias)** — Write down each significant decision: the options, the information you had, your reasoning, and the probability you assigned. Review months later and grade the *decision*, not the outcome.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">THREE DAILY HABITS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">One per bias you learned today</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"190\" width=\"800\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">1.</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">HUNT THE CEMETERY</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Find the failed cases before believing</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">any 'success habit' story</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"380\" width=\"800\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">2.</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">ASK WHO ISN'T HERE</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"470\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Reviews, surveys, polls — who is</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"505\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">missing from the sample?</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"570\" width=\"800\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"620\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">3.</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"620\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">KEEP A DECISION JOURNAL</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"660\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Record reasoning at the time —</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"695\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">then grade the process, not the result</text></svg>",
      "caption": "One habit per bias, starting today"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Spot the Bias",
     "content": "Read each scenario and identify which bias is at work — **survivorship, self-selection, or outcome**.\n\n**Scenario A:** A Yelp page for a restaurant has 200 reviews — 60% are 5-star, 30% are 1-star, 10% are in between.\n\n**Scenario B:** An NBA documentary interviews 12 players who shoot 1,000 free throws a day and concludes that volume practice is the secret to elite shooting.\n\n**Scenario C:** A board fires its CFO after a hedge fund attack drops the share price 20%, even though the CFO's strategy had been unanimously approved months earlier.\n\n*Pause before reading the answers below.*\n\n**Answers:** A = self-selection (extreme experiences post). B = survivorship (no data on the thousands who practiced and never made the NBA). C = outcome bias (the decision was endorsed when it was made; only the outcome changed the verdict).",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">PRACTICE EXERCISE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Identify the bias in each scenario</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"190\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">SCENARIO A — YELP REVIEWS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">U-shaped: 60% love, 30% hate, 10% meh</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"20\">Answer: Self-selection bias</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"380\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">SCENARIO B — NBA SHOOTERS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"475\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">12 pros all practice volume → secret?</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"515\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"20\">Answer: Survivorship bias</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"570\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"620\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">SCENARIO C — FIRED CFO</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"665\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Strategy approved then punished only</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"20\">Answer: Outcome bias</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Train the diagnostic muscle"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Build Your Cemetery List",
     "content": "Pick one belief you currently hold about success — career, fitness, investing, parenting, anything.\n\nWrite it out as a sentence beginning with: **'X works because successful people do Y.'**\n\nNow, for the next 10 minutes:\n\n• Find **three** documented cases of people who did Y and *failed*\n• Find **one** documented case of a person who succeeded *without* Y\n• Estimate, honestly, what percentage of people who tried Y achieved the success you imagine\n\nIf you cannot find any failures, that itself is the warning sign — the cemetery is invisible to you, which means survivorship bias is shaping your belief.\n\nDo this exercise once a week with a different belief. Within a month you will have stress-tested several of the assumptions running your decisions.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">CEMETERY EXERCISE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Stress-test a success belief in 10 minutes</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"190\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">STEP 1 — WRITE THE BELIEF</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"X works because winners do Y\"</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"340\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">STEP 2 — FIND 3 FAILURES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"435\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">People who did Y and still failed</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"490\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"540\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">STEP 3 — FIND 1 EXCEPTION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"585\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Someone who succeeded without Y</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"640\" width=\"800\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"690\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">STEP 4 — ESTIMATE BASE RATE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"735\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">% of Y-doers who actually succeeded</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Make survivorship bias visible in your own beliefs"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "A university posts a graph of alumni starting salaries based on responses to a voluntary mail-in survey, and reports an impressive average of $94,000. Which selection bias is the *biggest* threat to that number?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Survivorship bias — only living alumni responded",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Outcome bias — graduates judged by salary",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Self-selection bias — employed, higher-earning alumni are more motivated to respond",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Anchoring bias — graduates anchored on tuition costs",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Voluntary mail-in surveys suffer **self-selection bias**: alumni proud of their salaries are more likely to respond; the unemployed, underemployed, and embarrassed are more likely to ignore the survey. The reported average reflects who chose to participate, not the actual graduating class. This is identical in mechanism to the 1936 Literary Digest failure.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Voluntary surveys are dangerous data</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"200\" width=\"800\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.1)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">SELF-SELECTION</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Respondents differ systematically</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">from non-respondents</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"430\" width=\"800\" height=\"180\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.1)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"490\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">RESULT</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"540\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Average salary\" reflects pride,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"580\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">not the graduating cohort</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Who chose to reply? That's the real dataset"
     }
    }
