     "title": "The Linda Problem, Reframed",
     "content": "The clearest illustration of this debate is the famous Linda problem, one of Kahneman and Tversky's signature demonstrations. Subjects read a description of a fictional woman, Linda, as outspoken, philosophy-educated, and deeply concerned with social justice, then rank the probability of several statements about her. The overwhelming majority rank the statement that Linda is a bank teller and is active in the feminist movement as more probable than the plain statement that Linda is a bank teller, a logical impossibility, since any conjunction of two events cannot be more probable than either event alone. Kahneman and Tversky called this the conjunction fallacy and attributed it to the representativeness heuristic: the feminist-bank-teller description simply matches Linda's profile better, so it feels more probable, even though it cannot be. Gigerenzer's research team reran the identical experiment with one change: instead of asking subjects to rank single-event probabilities, they asked how many out of a hypothetical group of one hundred women fitting Linda's description would be bank tellers, versus feminist bank tellers. Framed this way, as a frequency count rather than an abstract probability judgment, the conjunction fallacy rate dropped dramatically, in some studies by more than half. Gigerenzer's interpretation is that human reasoning evolved to track natural frequencies encountered through lived experience, how many times has this happened out of how many opportunities, rather than to manipulate the single-event probability language that appears in academic experiments and textbooks, and that Kahneman-style studies may be measuring an unfamiliarity with a specific verbal format rather than a defect in reasoning itself. Kahneman's camp has responded that the fallacy, while reduced, does not vanish entirely under frequency framing, and that some genuine error remains. Both facts can be true at once, which is exactly what makes this a live scientific debate rather than a settled score.",
