     "title": "Holding the Whole Catalog with Humility",
     "content": "How should a careful student of critical thinking treat a book like Dobelli's, or any bias catalog, in light of this debate? Not by discarding it, and not by treating it as settled science either. First, use named biases as prompts for double-checking, not as verdicts of stupidity: when you notice a decision that resembles the sunk cost fallacy or the illusion of control, treat it as a flag worth a second look, not proof that your reasoning is broken. Second, calibrate your confidence to a finding's replication status rather than its popularity in bestselling books. A heuristic worth adopting is to be more confident in effects that have survived large, pre-registered, multi-lab replication attempts, as many framing and anchoring effects have, and more cautious about single-study findings that have not been retested at scale, as ego depletion had not, before it failed to replicate. Third, before labeling a real-world shortcut irrational, ask what environment it evolved or was trained for; a heuristic that fails an artificial laboratory puzzle may still be the best available tool for the noisy, information-scarce conditions of ordinary life, exactly as Gigerenzer's fast-and-frugal-heuristics research suggests. Fourth, resist the meta-bias of assuming any single framework, that humans are predictably irrational or that humans are ecologically rational, is the whole truth. The honest position, and the one this entire study guide has tried to model, is that both camps have identified real phenomena, disagree substantially about their interpretation, and that the debate itself is a working example of science self-correcting rather than a reason to dismiss the underlying research. Reading Dobelli well means taking his catalog seriously as a set of hypotheses about your own mind, while remaining equally skeptical of the catalog itself.",
